    },
    {
      "claim": "Microcystin-LR L4/L5 records show very low absorptive permeability (Papp_AB 2.2–4.8×10^-8 cm/s; C145, C153, C164, C165, C177, C188) and only 0.3–1.4% of loaded dose crossing in 24 h with apical re-excretion/efflux (C196, C204, C214, C218, C223, C230), supporting low absorption for a highly polar peptide-like molecule.",
      "card_ids": ["C145","C153","C164","C165","C177","C188","C196","C204","C214","C218","C223","C230"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 human jejunal perfusion for inogatran (C213) and Caco-2 non-permeability for 3,4,3-LI(1,2-HOPO) (C197, C203) provide additional examples of very low intestinal absorption for polar/ionized molecules.",
      "card_ids": ["C213","C197","C203"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Oseltamivir carboxylate/GS4071 low-F records (C05, C55, C57, C59, C85, C89) support low oral bioavailability of a polar carboxylate; the high F values for OC after oseltamivir (C10, C15, C26, C27, C32, C35, C38, C78) reflect systemic generation from the ester prodrug oseltamivir phosphate and do not transfer to the query, which has no prodrug conversion.",
      "card_ids": ["C05","C55","C57","C59","C85","C89","C10","C15","C26","C27","C32","C35","C38","C78"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic records are mostly for unrelated molecules; C284, C259, and C274 show reductive metabolism of α,β-unsaturated carboxylic acids in rat liver preparations, a motif present in the query's fumaramide arms, but applicability is low due to structural and species differences. No L6 record provides high metabolic stability for a query-like molecule.",
      "card_ids": ["C284","C259","C274"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C284","C259","C274"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability or pharmacokinetic measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific Caco-2/PAMPA, solubility, dissolution, gut-wall efflux, or hepatic metabolism data are available; L4-L6 records are for reference molecules.",
    "Transfer from acamprosate, microcystin-LR, inogatran, 3,4,3-LI(1,2-HOPO), and oseltamivir carboxylate is based on physicochemical and permeability-liability similarity rather than matched structure.",
    "Potential hepatic reductive metabolism of the fumaramide double bonds is speculative; no query-specific enzyme or hepatocyte data exist.",
    "No formulation or prodrug strategy data for the query are available."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low: the query is a fully ionized, highly polar DKP dicarboxylate (logD -5.3, TPSA 191 Å², 6 HBD/6 HBA, MW 452). Direct low-F references (acamprosate ≈11% in humans; oseltamivir carboxylate/GS4071 <5%) and L4/L5 permeability records (acamprosate Papp ~1.5×10^-7 cm/s; microcystin-LR Papp_AB 2.2–4.8×10^-8 cm/s with efflux; inogatran low human jejunal Peff) provide transferable experimental support for F<20%. L6 hepatic records are mostly not applicable; a few rat reduction records for α,β-unsaturated acids weakly support possible hepatic presystemic loss but do not change the decision. No record provides affirmative high permeability or favorable metabolism for a query-like molecule. Confidence remains high."
}

